Kent Paul Dolan wrote:
> Ohle Claussen wrote:
> > Rust attack. At this point, replacing the
> > unenchanted AC1 item with another unenchanted AC1
> > item is trivial and gets you back to starting point
> > at no cost.
>
> Sorry, but your whole analysis fails, because that word
> "trivial" is simply false.

At least for gloves, you're absolutely right.  That means using the
enchant first method is only at least as good (given worst-case
outcomes) as rustproofing.
Of course, when you're starting out you don't know whether the scrolls
are going to give +1,+2, or +3 AC, and while your item is +0,+1,or +2
all 3 are equally likely (and when it's +3, it's 50/50 whether you get
+1 or +2).

So the worst case (all +1s) is a 1/3 * 1/3 * 1/3 * 1/2 = 1/54 chance.
In that case (and assuming the gloves are already burnt or they get
burnt imediately) then your AC is only as good as it would be if you
fooproofed them first.

Or to phrase it differently, for base Ac1 armor over 98% of the time
the enchant-first method will be better (give you better AC for a time)
than the rustproof-first method even if the item is _already_ rusted.
Less than 2% of the time, enchant-first will only be just as good as
rustproof-first for already rusted items (If they're not already
rusted, this case will still give better AC until they rust, after
which the same AC)
Enchant-first will never be worse than rustproof-first.

> Moreover, fooproofing one item that looks keepable lets
> the player make the wise investment of concentrating
> available enchantments on that one piece of armor
> before turning to another.

You can do that whether you fooproof it or not. (although if you have
multiple keeper items it makes sense to enchant the ones that are less
than +3 first instead of maxing one out, since scrolls average +2 AC on
things under +3, but only +1.5 on things +3 or higher)

Just to clarify again:
armor damage only takes away up to the base AC of the item.  It doesn't
affect the enchantment.
So a rustproof +3 orcish helm is worth 4 AC (1 base, 3 enchantment)
a thoroughly rusty throughly corroded +3 orcish helm is worth 3 AC
(even though it has 6 steps of damage).

And items cannot be destroyed by this kind of damage.

When you foo-proof a piece of base ac1 armor, the only thing you're
doing is getting one point of AC (if it's damaged) or maybe stopping
from losing 1 point of AC in the future (if it isn't).
The tradeoff for this is not getting an average of +2 AC (with a
minimum of +1) by enchanting it.

Trading off either a guaranteed +1 (for already damaged items) or a
guaranteed worse-than +1 (+0 now but +1 in the future if the item would
have been damaged) for an average of +2 with a worst-case of +1 is a
clear loss.



